  {
   "kind": 1,
   "value": "### 8 — Conclusions (2026-10-09)\n1. **Compact exact form.** For any site-1 covector $\\sum_\\nu c_\\nu x_\\nu\\otimes e_1^T$, the overlap over $\\langle x_0|\\Psi_B\\rangle$ is the polynomial in $(\\alpha,\\beta,\\lambda_1,\\lambda_2)$ of cell 61 (exact, $10^{-47}$, all 64 states). It is $\\sum_x\\varphi^{(1)}_x\\psi_x+\\lambda_2\\sum_x\\varphi^{(2)}_x\\psi_x$ with $\\varphi^{(i)}=c\\cdot K^{(i)}$ ($K^{\\rm vac}$ rank 8, $K^{20}$ rank 4).\n2. **Neither group is a rotation**: $X(c\\cdot K^{(i)})$ has three nonzero singular values for generic rotations $c$. So no site-2 group is an L=1-type determinant with transformed bra data.\n3. **The L=1 $\\det_3\\times\\det_2$ is not a polynomial identity.** On ladder 1 of the L=2 states it is not even in the span of the 8 site-1 wavefunctions (0.11). At L=1 every function of the 8 states is in that span, which is why it works there.\n4. **Locality classifies the usable determinants.** For the L=1-type $3\\times3$, a ladder-1 bracket determinant is a site-1 object iff $F_2\\propto F_1$ on $\\{\\theta_1-2h,\\theta_1-h\\}$ (exact). The resulting family has a **4-dimensional image** (explicit cubic map, ideal of 10 quadrics); three independent bra functions do not enlarge it (max rank 4 over 32 components). The groups of generic rotations are not in it (exact Gröbner membership: 0.16–3.9 vs 0 for members; numeric fits 0.18, 0.014 vs $10^{-17}$ for a member).\n5. **Sums of two local $3\\times3$ determinants cover the whole 8-dim local space** (random vectors fit to $10^{-15}$), so a 'sum of four determinants' form exists for every site-1 covector and is uninformative.\n\n**Verdict.** Within ladder-1 bracket determinants of the natural type, the site-1 rotation has no single-determinant form; the exact representation is the 12-state sum in the compact form of item 1. A single-determinant form, if it exists, must use a different structure: brackets involving $v(\\theta_1+h)$ (outside the 2-plane), both ladders with insertions producing $\\lambda_2$, or larger determinants with locality by cancellation.",
   "languageId": "markdown",
   "outputs": [],
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "kind": 1,
   "value": "### 8b. The site-1 rotation as a sum over SoV points with an explicit bra wavefunction\nParametrize the rotation by $X(\\Phi)=a\\,b^T$ with $b\\cdot a=0$ ($X(x_0)=3e_{31}$, so $x_0\\leftrightarrow a=e_3,\\ b=e_1$ up to scale). Then\n$$\\frac{\\langle\\Phi|\\Psi_B\\rangle}{\\langle x_0|\\Psi_B\\rangle}=\\sum_{x\\in\\text{12 states}}\\Phi_g(x)\\,\\psi_B(x),\\qquad \\psi_B(x)=\\psi^{(1)}_{x_1}(B)\\,\\lambda_2(B)^{[x_2=\\{2|20\\}]},$$\nwith $\\Phi_g(x)$ a bilinear form in $(a,b)$ with rational coefficients (at $t=\\theta_{12}/h=62/35$), listed below per SoV state. The coefficients' $t$-dependence is Direction 4.",
   "languageId": "markdown",
   "outputs": [],
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "kind": 2,
   "value": "(* c-vector of the rotation X = a b^T, symbolic a, b (the map is linear) *)\naS = {a1, a2, a3}; bS = {b1, b2, b3};\ncAB = Simplify[rotFromAB[aS, bS]];\n(* consistency: X(c(a,b)) reproduces a b^T up to the traceless projection (b.a = 0 is imposed by hand below) *)\nchecks[\"X(c(a,b)) = a b^T  (with b.a = 0)\"] = Simplify[Xc[cAB] - Outer[Times, aS, bS] /. b3 -> -(a1 b1 + a2 b2)/a3] === ConstantArray[0, {3, 3}];\nΦvac = Simplify[cAB . Kvac /. b3 -> -(a1 b1 + a2 b2)/a3]; Φ20 = Simplify[cAB . K20 /. b3 -> -(a1 b1 + a2 b2)/a3];\nbraTab = Join[Table[{\"site 1 \" <> ToString[trip[[x]]] <> \", site 2 vac\", Factor[Φvac[[x]]]}, {x, 8}], Table[{\"site 1 \" <> ToString[trip[[x]]] <> \", site 2 {2|20}\", Factor[Φ20[[x]]]}, {x, 8}]];\nbraTab = Select[braTab, #[[2]] =!= 0 &];\n{checks[\"X(c(a,b)) = a b^T  (with b.a = 0)\"], Grid[braTab, Frame -> All, Alignment -> Left]}",
   "languageId": "wolfram",
   "outputs": [
    {
     "items": [
