   "kind": 1,
   "value": "## 11. Beyond the $(w_1,y)$ family: the ket-twist torus (2026-10-09, later)\n\n**Question (Paul).** Can the construction of §10 be generalized to states beyond the vacuum, i.e. more general rotations on each of the two sites?\n\n**Tool.** The Cartan torus of the ket twist, $\\rho(D)\\otimes\\rho(D)$ with $D=\\mathrm{diag}(d_1,d_2,d_3)$, commutes with every twisted transfer matrix and acts on an eigenstate $\\Psi_B$ of total weight $\\mu$ by the character $\\chi_\\mu(D)=\\prod_i d_i^{\\mu_i}$ (for a resolvent $D=(y_1-G^T)^{-1}$ this character is the su(2)-like $\\det_4$ of §2). So every product state with a determinant overlap comes with its whole torus orbit for free: $\\langle v_1\\rho(D)\\otimes v_2\\rho(D)|\\Psi_B\\rangle=\\chi_\\mu(D)\\,\\langle v_1\\otimes v_2|\\Psi_B\\rangle$ (cell 108).\n\n**Expectation to test.** The §10 family is $v_2=\\Omega_{(w_1,y)}$, $v_1=v_2\\,\\rho(D_{w_2(y)})$ with $w_2(y)=y\\,(\\theta_{12}-2h)/(\\theta_{12}-h)$; its torus orbit is $\\{(g\\,\\rho(D_{w_2(y)}),\\,g)\\}$ with $g=\\Omega_{(w_1,y)}\\rho(D)$. If $(w_1,D)\\mapsto\\Omega_{(w_1,y)}\\rho(D)$ at fixed $y$ were dominant onto the 3-dimensional rotation orbit, every rotation $g$ would be $\\Omega_{(w_1,y)}\\rho(D)$ with $y$ free, and $(g\\rho(D_\\kappa),g)$ would have a determinant formula for every $\\kappa$ — the paper's $\\kappa$-family. **It is not** (cell 108): at fixed $y$ the Jacobian has rank 3 = torus + scale, i.e. $w_1$ is itself a torus direction ($\\Omega_{(w_1,y)}/\\Omega_{(w_1',y)}$ is a torus character, exactly) and $y$ is a torus **invariant** of the rotation. Hence every rotation $g$ on site 2 is $\\Omega_{(w_1,y(g))}\\rho(D)$ with $y(g)$ unique, and its site-1 partner $g\\,\\rho((w_2(y(g))-G^T)^{-1})$ is unique:\n$$\\langle g\\,\\rho((w_2(y(g))-G^T)^{-1})\\otimes g\\,|\\Psi_B\\rangle \\ \\propto\\ \\chi_\\mu(D)\\ \\frac{\\det_6N[Q_B,Q_{\\rm vac,(w_1,w_2(y(g)))}]}{\\Psi_Q}\\ \\frac{\\det_2^{(1)}[Q_B;y(g)]}{\\mathrm{minor}_1}.$$\n\nCells: 108 character + dominance (exact); 109 the $(w_1,y)$ family at fresh $w_1$; 110 iteration with the §10 product state as seed; 111 a generic rotation $g$ end to end; 112 result.",
   "languageId": "markdown",
   "outputs": [],
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "kind": 2,
   "value": "(* 11.1 (a) the ket-twist torus is the weight character: ρdiag[d]⊗ρdiag[d] is diagonal on every eigenstate Ψ_B with eigenvalue χ_μ(d) = Π d_i^μ_i (μ = total weight, muS), for ANY pair of covectors. For a resolvent d = 1/(y1 - z) this is the su(2)-like det4 of cell 10 (pref). Checked on random covectors to fix the convention (ρdiag acts on covectors from the right, exponent +μ); difference norm, not a ratio, since a random product covector can have zero overlap with a state. *)\ncharB[d_, B_] := Times @@ (d^muS[[B]]);\nSeedRandom[11]; φr = RandomInteger[{-5, 5}, d1]; ψr = RandomInteger[{-5, 5}, d1]; dr = {3/7, -5/4, 2/9};\nchecks[\"11.1a torus = character on random covectors (max |lhs - χ rhs| / max |lhs|)\"] = Module[{lhs, rhs}, lhs = Table[Flatten[KroneckerProduct[φr . ρdiag[dr], ψr . ρdiag[dr]]] . vecS[[B]], {B, nS}]; rhs = Table[charB[dr, B] (Flatten[KroneckerProduct[φr, ψr]] . vecS[[B]]), {B, nS}]; Max[Abs[lhs - rhs]]/Max[Abs[lhs]]];\n(* (b) dominance (exact, symbolic): the rotation orbit of x0 is 3-dim projectively. The map (w1, d1, d2, d3) -> Ω_{(w1,y)}.ρdiag[d] at FIXED y has 4 parameters including the scale (ρdiag[c d] = c^|Λ| ρdiag[d]), so it is dominant onto the orbit iff its Jacobian has rank 4; the torus alone gives rank 3 (2 + scale). With y also free: rank 4 means the image is the whole orbit; the null vector of the 5-column Jacobian is the fibre tangent. Generic points only: Ω_{(w1,y)} degenerates at y = z_i (the first run sat at y = 5/4 = z2). *)\nΩdS = oneSiteVac[zN, {w1s, yys, 3/5}] . ρdiag[{d1s, d2s, d3s}];\njacAt[vars_, pt_] := D[ΩdS, {vars}] /. pt;\nptsR = {{w1s -> -7/3, yys -> 7/5, d1s -> 3/7, d2s -> -5/4, d3s -> 2/9}, {w1s -> 9/4, yys -> -2/3, d1s -> -1/3, d2s -> 4/7, d3s -> 5/2}, {w1s -> 13/8, yys -> 11/9, d1s -> 2/5, d2s -> 7/3, d3s -> -3/4}};\nrankTab = Table[{MatrixRank[jacAt[{w1s, d1s, d2s, d3s}, pt]], MatrixRank[jacAt[{w1s, yys, d1s, d2s, d3s}, pt]], MatrixRank[jacAt[{d1s, d2s, d3s}, pt]], With[{ns = NullSpace[jacAt[{w1s, yys, d1s, d2s, d3s}, pt]]}, If[Length[ns] == 1, ns[[1]]/ns[[1, 1]], ns]]}, {pt, ptsR}];\nchecks[\"11.1b ranks {fixed y, y free, torus alone} at 3 generic points\"] = rankTab[[All, 1 ;; 3]];\nchecks[\"11.1b fibre tangent (w1, y, d1, d2, d3), normalized to w1-component 1\"] = rankTab[[All, 4]];\n(* consequence test: is w1 a torus direction, i.e. Ω_{(w1,y)} = Ω_{(w1',y)} ρ(D) for some D? If the fibre has no y-component, yes. Direct check: rank of {Ω_{(w1,y)}, Ω_{(w1',y)}} after dividing out the torus = is Ω_{(w1,y)}/Ω_{(w1',y)} componentwise a character? *)\ntorusQuot[y_, w1a_, w1b_] := Module[{va = oneSiteVac[zN, {w1a, y, 3/5}], vb = oneSiteVac[zN, {w1b, y, 3/5}], rat, dd}, rat = va/vb; (* a torus element multiplies component p by Π d_k^J0[k][p]; solve d from three components and verify the rest *)\n   dd = Table[Product[dS[k]^J0[k][p], {k, 3}], {p, ps[{Λ}]}]; Simplify[Union[rat - (dd /. First[Solve[Thread[dd[[{1, 2, 4}]] == rat[[{1, 2, 4}]]], {dS[1], dS[2], dS[3]}]])]]];\nchecks[\"11.1c Ω_{(w1,y)} vs Ω_{(w1',y)}: componentwise ratio is a torus character (residuals)\"] = {torusQuot[7/5, -7/3, 9/4], torusQuot[-2/3, 13/8, 5/2]};\nGrid[{{\"torus = character, random covectors (rel dev)\", N[checks[\"11.1a torus = character on random covectors (max |lhs - χ rhs| / max |lhs|)\"], 3]}, {\"ranks {fixed y, y free, torus alone} at 3 points\", checks[\"11.1b ranks {fixed y, y free, torus alone} at 3 generic points\"]}, {\"fibre tangents (w1, y, d1, d2, d3)\", N[checks[\"11.1b fibre tangent (w1, y, d1, d2, d3), normalized to w1-component 1\"], 4]}, {\"Ω_{(w1,y)} / Ω_{(w1',y)} a torus character? (residuals)\", checks[\"11.1c Ω_{(w1,y)} vs Ω_{(w1',y)}: componentwise ratio is a torus character (residuals)\"]}}, Frame -> All, Alignment -> Left]",
   "languageId": "wolfram",
   "outputs": [
    {
     "items": [
